.TH BLOCKTOOLS 7 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.1.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
blocktools \- offline wallet recovery, inspection and signing tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\&./blocktools run '<pipeline>'
\&./blocktools reveal '<pipeline>'
recipes/recover.sh FILE DEST [options]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Small tools joined by JSON lines on stdin and stdout. Readers turn wallet files into keys, derive and key\-to\-addresses turn keys into addresses, tx\-build/tx\-verify/tx\-sign/tx\-broadcast move funds. Every signature and broadcast waits for a code typed on the terminal.
.PP
Run anything that touches secrets inside ./blocktools run (sealed, offline). Policy files: docs/POLICY.md. Supported chains: docs/CHAINS.md. Start here: docs/getting\-started.md.
.SH OPTIONS
See blocktools(1) and each tool's man page. The wrapper passes the pipeline to sealed\-run; tools parse their own flags.
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
Pipelines speak JSONL. The sealed boundary redacts secret fields before host\-visible stdout. Host\-side network tools (utxo\-fetch, tx\-broadcast, xmr\-wallet) see addresses and signed transactions only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Pipeline finished.
.TP
2
Tool or boundary error.
.TP
3
Refused (watch\-only, policy, human abort).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
\&./blocktools run 'classify < /in/seed.txt'
recipes/recover.sh /in/seed.txt bc1q... \-\-goal addresses
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
DANGEROUS with real funds. Audit the code. Prefer sealed offline runs. See docs/SECURITY.md.
